1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Listen to a short talk on architecture.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,780
Before starting our tour of Monticello,

3
00:00:03,780 --> 00:00:09,100
I'd like to give you some historical facts that might help you appreciate what you see today even more.

4
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:14,610
Monticello was the very much loved home of Thomas Jefferson for over fifty years.

5
00:00:14,610 --> 00:00:17,360
Jefferson, who was, of course, President

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:20,120
was also a great reader and language enthusiast.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,791
He read widely on different subjects, including architecture.

8
00:00:22,791 --> 00:00:26,320
He wasn't formally trained in architecture

9
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,801
but as a result of his study and observation of other buildings,

10
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:32,140
he was able to help design and build the house.

11
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,860
He chose the site himself, naming the estate "Monticello

12
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:38,200
which means "little mountain" in Italian.

13
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,635
In fact, many of the ideas behind the design also came from the Italian architect Andrea Palladio,

14
00:00:43,635 --> 00:00:46,440
who lived in the sixteenth century and

15
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:49,070
who had a great influence on the architecture of England.

16
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:52,500
Jefferson, however, ignored one of Palladio's principles

17
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,829
that is, not to build in a high place.

18
00:00:55,829 --> 00:00:59,320
Monticello's elevation made the transportation of what was needed at the house

19
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:03,010
for example, food --- especially difficult.

20
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:05,880
But the view from the estate would not be as spectacular

21
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:08,221
if Jefferson had followed Palladio's advice;

22
00:01:08,221 --> 00:01:12,206
there really is no boundary between the house and the nature around it,

23
00:01:12,206 --> 00:01:18,030
and so Jefferson was able to look out on his beloved state of Virginia from his wonderful vantage point.

24
00:01:18,030 --> 00:01:20,220
Now we'll go on to Jefferson's library.
